{"bug":"K001","report":"C","v":"missed","ref":"Major finding 3: Dropped read operations can permanently lose expiration timers","evidence":"“A full read-operation queue discards the operation carrying the timer update.” This concerns lost expiration timers, not a queued Upsert re-admitting a removed entry and leaving an unreachable deque node that stalls eviction or leaks capacity."}
{"bug":"K001","report":"D","v":"missed","ref":"Entire report, findings 1–5","evidence":"“the registered waiter remains `Computing`” concerns initialization after a weigher panic. No finding identifies stale queued Upsert admission, an unreachable deque node, or the resulting eviction stall or capacity leak."}
{"bug":"K004","report":"C","v":"missed","ref":"Entire report, findings 1–6","evidence":"“another computation can read the same old value” concerns concurrent compute serialization. The report does not identify a completed failed try_get_with retaining its error and preventing a sequential same-key, same-error-type initializer from running."}
{"bug":"K005","report":"D","v":"found","ref":"Finding 1: Async computations release serialization before applying the operation","evidence":"“`set_waiter_value` consumes the guard and removes the waiter before the subsequent insertion or removal” and “Both computations read `0`; the final value is `1`, expected `2`.” This identifies premature waiter release in the future compute path, stale reads, and a lost update."}
